# C256 two-bank B-prefetch experiment

This is an uncompiled private draft. It changes the register schedule of the
existing vector-cache variant 3, which uses ascending K32 MMAs and coalesced
packed output stores. It has no production dispatch or policy entry.

The v25 rolling-prefetch SASS contains eight enabled carry `MOV`s at each of
seven K handoffs in nine dense loops: 504 per warp/window. That version loads
the next eight B words early, then copies them into the current bank. Its
[CPU SASS analysis](../outputs/window_vector_prefetch_v25_analysis.json)
establishes the instruction cost, not its runtime significance. The draft
removes those assignments from the source. A new SASS audit must establish
whether the compiler actually removes the copies without introducing spills
or an equivalent register shuffle.

## Schedule and unchanged arithmetic

Two separately named arrays, `b_even` and `b_odd`, each hold eight B words.
There is no dynamically indexed bank array and no bank-to-bank assignment.

```text
load K0 into even; load K1 into odd
for K = 0, 2, 4, 6:
    compute K from even
    if K+2 exists: load K+2 into even
    compute K+1 from odd
    if K+3 exists: load K+3 into odd
```

The K2 load occurs before K1 computes; K3 loads before K2 computes. This
continues across every pair boundary. The earlier pair experiment restarted
loads at each pair and therefore did not preserve that overlap. The final pair
computes K6/K7 without requesting K8/K9.

Each accumulator still receives K0 through K7 exactly once, in that order.
Each K step reads the same four shared A fragments and executes the same
sixteen MMAs. The source requests the same sixteen vector B loads per dense
loop. Nine dense calls change: four W1 chunks, W3, Q/K/V separately, and the
attention projection. W2 and attention's own MMAs remain untouched. All seven
shared-memory barriers, publication rounding, residual arithmetic, cache
preparation, and output addressing are unchanged.

The generator replaces only the rolled `dense32` body and the private symbol
names. Reversing that replacement makes all executable kernel text identical
to the vector-cache baseline. Unlike the independent QKV experiment, this
draft does not reuse A across Q, K and V or change their accumulator layout.
See the [v26 QKV audit](../profile/qkv-sass-v26-cpu-20261003T055001Z/REPORT.md)
for that separate comparison.

## Files and API

All new source files end in `.draft`; the normal build does not compile them.

- `tools/draft_window_pingpong_prefetch.py.draft` reproduces the three C++ drafts.
- `csrc/kernel_impl/window_block_pingpong_prefetch.cuh.draft` contains the kernel.
- `csrc/kernel_launcher/window_block_pingpong_prefetch.cu.draft` preserves the
  existing cache validation, alignment copies, empty-batch handling and stream.
- `csrc/torch_api/window_block_pingpong_prefetch.cpp.draft` registers the private op.
- `tools/window_pingpong_prefetch_proof.py.draft` and
  `tests/test_window_pingpong_schedule.py.draft` check the CPU trace and addresses.
- `tests/test_window_pingpong_prefetch.py.draft` holds GPU numerical/capture tests.
- `tests/benchmark_window_pingpong_prefetch.py.draft` holds the matched-contract
  component benchmark.

The private schema is
`_inference_window_block_pingpong_prefetch(state, w1, w2, w3, qkv_weight,
projection_weight, ffn_scale, attn_scale, head_scale, bias, phase=0,
packed_output=True, raw_output=False) -> (published, raw)`.

It accepts C256 FP8 inference, with half or packed-byte input storage and
packed-byte published output. Optional raw output is half. It uses the same
dual weight caches as `_prepare_window_vector_weight`. Half input storage is
not a separate FP16-arithmetic mode. Unsupported channels, nonpacked output,
wrong cache shapes/devices/dtypes, and gradient requests are rejected. It does
not introduce an autograd path or alter the training implementations.

## Validation and measurement plan

The CPU schedule proof covers trip counts 1 through 9, including the deployed
eight. It checks both initial loads, aligned/in-bounds matrix addresses,
exactly-once B loads, ascending A/MMA order, bank lifetime, and lookahead across
pair boundaries. Deliberately broken schedules must fail those checks.

The GPU test draft covers all four phases, spatial tails, batch 2, half/packed
input, optional raw output, misalignment and noncontiguous operands, empty
batch, real checkpoint blocks 15/49, immutable inputs/caches, and capture
replay after input/scale/cache mutation and output poisoning. It compares the
result with independent composition, all four canonical and four cache
variants, the earlier pair experiment, v25 rolling B-prefetch, and both v26
QKV tile sizes. These tests have not been run for this draft.

The benchmark times fourteen paths using distinct-output CUDA graphs, an
output-memory budget, deterministic shuffled order, and a reverse-order
option. Caches are resident for every cache-based path; preparation is excluded
consistently and recorded. It checks bytes and input immutability before
accepting a timing, plus poisoned replay for the new and recent private paths.
Reports include the installed binary/device metadata and source hashes.
The new path must beat the fastest measured existing path by more than 3%; a
win against only the old canonical kernel is insufficient.

After explicit activation and compilation by the root agent, suggested runs are:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest tests/test_window_pingpong_prefetch.py -q
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/benchmark_window_pingpong_prefetch.py --output outputs/window_pingpong_trial.json
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe tests/benchmark_window_pingpong_prefetch.py --reverse --output outputs/window_pingpong_trial_reverse.json
```

The default benchmark covers blocks 15/49 at 48×84, 72×120 and 136×240, phase 1,
packed input/output and no raw side output. Other storage/raw/phase contracts
require explicit additional runs before any broader selection claim.

The next SASS review must verify eight ascending K steps, exactly one load for
each B fragment, cross-pair lookahead, disappearance of the carry moves, no
local traffic, and unchanged shared/output traffic. Pairing doubles the loop
body and can increase code size, register pressure or instruction-cache cost.
The compiler can also schedule loads later than the source suggests. None of
those outcomes can be settled by the CPU trace. GPU byte parity, timing,
original-implementation speed, and the 85% roofline gate remain unverified for
this draft.
